// Brought to u by MidManStudio
//
// One combined patch, 16 anchored edits. It carries the D3b sema half (3 ops) that was committed twice
// but never run, and the D2 sema half (13 ops), so a single run of the "Apply Patches" workflow brings
// master level with the emitter and the fixtures that are already on it.
//
// D3b (docs/ARENA_STRINGS.md), crates/core/src/sema/type_infer.rs, 3 ops: one new helper, one line in
// the interpolation literal arm, one line at the top of binop_result (the helper now guards the error
// type: an operand that already failed made the first version index past the type table, found by the
// rd_parser test `an_operator_on_an_expression_that_already_failed_reports_nothing_more`). An interpolated string written
// inside `with arena` is an arena value, so sema refuses to let it outlive the block; `==`, `<` and
// `+` still read it. Fixes the err_arena_string_* fixtures and examples/20_arena_strings.
//
// D2 (docs/LLVM_POC.md, "The tier of a closure"), TIER-017 LowCallsUnknownClosure, 13 ops: a LOW
// function may call a closure only if it is an immutable `let` bound straight to a lambda in that
// function; a parameter, a `mut` binding or a closure from a call is refused. MID and HIGH may call
// any closure. The emitter already applies the same rule. 5 ops in errors/tier/mod.rs (the variant,
// its span, message, suggestion and code), 6 in sema/tier_check.rs (a field, its initialiser, a note
// at each `let`, the hook in the Call arm for identifier callees, the new check_closure_call, the rules
// list at the top),
// 2 in docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md (the registry row and the entry). Fixes err_closure_tier_*.
//
// Every anchor is ASCII and was checked to match exactly one line of master (6b2f837) with the same
// normalisation the applier uses; the ops are independent of each other in order. If the other chats
// have moved an anchor, that op prints an error and is skipped and nothing is overwritten silently,
// but then the ops of the same group will not all have applied: run the workflow with dry_run on first
// and read the "patched : N" line, which must say 16.

@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_insert_after<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "insert_after", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
  ~patch_insert_before<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "insert_before", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
  ~patch_replace_regex<object>(_path, _pattern, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_regex", pattern = _pattern, content = _content }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_insert_before("crates/core/src/sema/type_infer.rs", "/// Wrap `inner_ty` as an ArenaRef if inside a `with arena` block.", "    /// D3b (docs/ARENA_STRINGS.md): if `ty` is an arena-tagged string (`&arena string`), the plain\n    /// string type; any other type comes back unchanged. For an operator that only reads its string\n    /// operands (`==`, `<`, `+`): the result is a `bool`, or a new string that is always allocated in\n    /// the GC heap (a copy), so it carries no borrow of the operand. An operand that already failed has\n    /// the error type, which is not in the type table, so it comes back unchanged.\n    fn peel_arena_str(&mut self, ty: TypeId) -> TypeId {\n        let resolved = self.apply(ty);\n        if resolved == TypeId::ERROR {\n            return ty;\n        }\n        if let SemaType::ArenaRef { inner, .. } = self.ctx.types.get(resolved).clone() {\n            let inner = self.apply(inner);\n            if inner != TypeId::ERROR && matches!(self.ctx.types.get(inner), SemaType::Str) {\n                return inner;\n            }\n        }\n        ty\n    }\n\n")
    patch_replace_regex("crates/core/src/sema/type_infer.rs", "(self\\.check_format_spec\\(spec, hole_ty, expr\\.span\\);\\s*\\}\\s*\\}\\s*\\}\\s*)self\\.ctx\\.types\\.intern\\(SemaType::Str\\)", "\\g<1>// D3b: an interpolated string is allocated where it is written (docs/ARENA_STRINGS.md).\n                let str_ty = self.ctx.types.intern(SemaType::Str);\n                self.maybe_arena_ref(str_ty)")
    patch_insert_after("crates/core/src/sema/type_infer.rs", "fn binop_result(&mut self, op: BinOp, lhs: TypeId, rhs: TypeId, span: Span) -> TypeId {", "        let (lhs, rhs) = (self.peel_arena_str(lhs), self.peel_arena_str(rhs));\n")
    patch_insert_before("crates/core/src/error_management/errors/tier/mod.rs", "Trait method tiers", "    // ── Closures ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    /// A `@tier(low)` function called a closure whose tier it cannot know (decision D2,\n    /// docs/LLVM_POC.md, \"The tier of a closure\"). A closure has the tier of the function it is\n    /// created in, and a function type carries no tier, so the only closure a LOW function can\n    /// know to be LOW is one it made itself: a local bound by an immutable `let` straight to a\n    /// lambda. A parameter, a `mut` binding or a closure that came back from a call could belong\n    /// to a higher tier, and LOW runs nothing that is not LOW. MID and HIGH may call any closure.\n    LowCallsUnknownClosure {\n        name:   String,\n        reason: String,\n        span:   Span,\n    },\n\n")
    patch_insert_after("crates/core/src/error_management/errors/tier/mod.rs", "TierError::TraitMethodTierMismatch { span, .. } => *span,", "            TierError::LowCallsUnknownClosure { span, .. } => *span,\n")
    patch_insert_before("crates/core/src/error_management/errors/tier/mod.rs", "TierError::StaticAccessOutsideHigh { name, actual, .. } =>", "            TierError::LowCallsUnknownClosure { name, reason, .. } =>\n                format!(\n                    \"`@tier(low)` code cannot call the closure `{}`: {}\",\n                    name, reason\n                ),\n\n")
    patch_insert_before("crates/core/src/error_management/errors/tier/mod.rs", "TierError::OwnershipWrapperOutsideLowTier { wrapper, .. } =>", "            TierError::LowCallsUnknownClosure { name, .. } =>\n                Some(format!(\n                    \"bind the closure in this function with `let {} = fn(...) ...` (not `mut`), \\\n                     or call it from a `@tier(mid)` or `@tier(high)` function\",\n                    name\n                )),\n\n")
    patch_insert_after("crates/core/src/error_management/errors/tier/mod.rs", "TierError::TraitMethodTierMismatch { .. } => \"TIER-016\",", "            TierError::LowCallsUnknownClosure { .. }         => \"TIER-017\",\n")
    patch_insert_after("crates/core/src/sema/tier_check.rs", "//!   (`TIER-015`)", "//! - a `@tier(low)` function may call only a closure it created itself: a local bound by an\n//!   immutable `let` straight to a lambda in that function (`TIER-017`, decision D2)\n")
    patch_insert_after("crates/core/src/sema/tier_check.rs", "errors:       &'a mut ErrorManager,", "    /// D2: the statement span of every immutable `let` bound straight to a lambda. Name resolution\n    /// records a `let`'s local with that span as its `defined_at`, so a call of a local can be\n    /// matched to one of these (see `check_closure_call`).\n    lambda_lets:  std::collections::HashSet<Span>,\n")
    patch_replace_text("crates/core/src/sema/tier_check.rs", "TierChecker { ctx, errors, current_tier: TierAnnotation::High }", "TierChecker { ctx, errors, current_tier: TierAnnotation::High, lambda_lets: std::collections::HashSet::new() }")
    patch_insert_after("crates/core/src/sema/tier_check.rs", "fn check_stmt<'ast>(&mut self, stmt: &Stmt<'ast>) {", "        // D2: note an immutable `let` bound straight to a lambda, so that a call of that local from a\n        // LOW function is known to call a closure LOW made itself (see `check_closure_call`).\n        if let StmtKind::Let {\n            mutable: false,\n            binding: crate::ast::statements::BindingTarget::Ident(_),\n            value,\n            ..\n        } = &stmt.kind\n        {\n            if matches!(value.kind, ExprKind::Lambda(_)) {\n                self.lambda_lets.insert(stmt.span);\n            }\n        }\n")
    patch_insert_after("crates/core/src/sema/tier_check.rs", "ExprKind::Call { callee, args } => {", "                // D2: a call of a local or a parameter by its plain name calls a closure. Only an\n                // identifier callee is looked at: the emitter refuses any other callee in LOW, and a\n                // span can be shared by unrelated nodes in a hand-built tree.\n                if let ExprKind::Ident(_) = callee.kind {\n                    if let Some(callee_def_id) = self.ctx.resolutions.get(callee.span) {\n                        if callee_def_id.0 < self.ctx.symbols.len() {\n                            self.check_closure_call(callee_def_id, expr.span);\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n")
    patch_insert_before("crates/core/src/sema/tier_check.rs", "/// Look up a callee's tier from the symbol table and enforce cross-tier rules.", "    /// D2 (docs/LLVM_POC.md, \"The tier of a closure\"). A call whose callee is a local or a parameter\n    /// calls a closure. A closure has the tier of the function it is created in and a function type\n    /// carries no tier, so a LOW function may call one only if it made it itself: a local bound by an\n    /// immutable `let` straight to a lambda in this function (a lambda written inside it is walked at\n    /// the same tier and may call it too). A parameter, a `mut` binding, a closure that came back from\n    /// a call (even from a LOW function) and anything else could belong to a higher tier. MID and HIGH\n    /// may call any closure: handing one down to a MID function that calls it is the callback pattern,\n    /// so the by-name rule of `tier_call_forbidden` is deliberately not applied to closures.\n    fn check_closure_call(&mut self, callee_def_id: DefId, call_span: Span) {\n        let def = self.ctx.symbols.lookup(callee_def_id);\n        let reason = match &def.kind {\n            DefKind::Param { .. } => {\n                \"it is a parameter, and a function type carries no tier, so it could be a closure from a higher tier\"\n            }\n            DefKind::Local { mutable: true } => {\n                \"it is a `mut` binding, which can be assigned a closure from a higher tier\"\n            }\n            DefKind::Local { mutable: false } => {\n                if self.lambda_lets.contains(&def.defined_at) {\n                    return;\n                }\n                \"it was not bound by a `let` straight to a lambda in this function, so its tier is not known\"\n            }\n            _ => return,\n        };\n        if self.current_tier == TierAnnotation::Low {\n            self.errors.add_tier_error(TierError::LowCallsUnknownClosure {\n                name:   def.name.clone(),\n                reason: reason.to_string(),\n                span:   call_span,\n            });\n        }\n    }\n\n")
    patch_insert_after("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "| TIER-016 | TraitMethodTierMismatch |", "| TIER-017 | LowCallsUnknownClosure |\n")
    patch_insert_after("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "`TIER-016` `TraitMethodTierMismatch`", "- `TIER-017` `LowCallsUnknownClosure` — *Error*. \"`@tier(low)` code cannot call the closure `f`: {reason}\" — docs/LLVM_POC.md, \"The tier of a closure\" (decision D2). Suggestion: bind the closure in this function with `let f = fn(...) ...` (not `mut`), or call it from a `@tier(mid)` or `@tier(high)` function. Fires in `tier_check.rs` for a call whose callee is a local or a parameter, inside a `@tier(low)` function or method, unless the callee is an immutable `let` bound straight to a lambda in that function (a lambda written inside that function may call it too). A closure has the tier of the function it is created in and a function type carries no tier, so a parameter, a `mut` binding, a closure returned by a call (even from a LOW function) and anything else could belong to a higher tier, and LOW runs nothing that is not LOW. `@tier(mid)` and `@tier(high)` code may call any closure (handing one down to a MID function that calls it is the callback pattern), so the by-name rule of `TIER-005` is deliberately not applied to closures. The emitter in `crates/llvm_poc` applies the same rule independently. Pinned by `tests/fixtures/*_closure_tier_*`.\n")
)
